================================================================================
3. THIRD-PARTY SOURCE  —  NOT LICENSED HERE.  READ THIS BEFORE REDISTRIBUTING.
================================================================================

**This licence does not grant you any right to the source code of the audited
protocols, and it cannot.**

The dataset quotes Solidity source from eight public Code4rena audits. Those
repositories are licensed by their authors, not by us. Most declare **no
licence at all**; several are BUSL-1.1 or carry the Commons Clause. Quotation
for research and citation is a different question from redistribution, and that
question is the downstream user's to answer — we cannot answer it for you and we
have not obtained permission from those authors.

Concretely, as measured by `scripts/audit_third_party.py` and recorded per record
in `derived/records.jsonl`:

  * records whose `redistribution.flag` is
    `source_redistributed_without_declared_license` contain verbatim source text
    quoted from a repository that declares no licence. **If you intend to
    redistribute this dataset, or to train a model on it and ship the model or
    its outputs, you must decide for yourself whether that use is lawful in your
    jurisdiction. We make no representation that it is.**
  * records whose `redistribution.flag` is `withheld` contain **no** source text.
    The citation survives: path, commit, line range, URL and sha256.
    `scripts/fetch_sources.py` retrieves the file locally so its structure can be
    analysed without copying it out of the repository.
  * PoC code in `jsonl/exploits.jsonl` belongs to the Code4rena submission
    authors. It is redistributed as quoted evidence, with its original SPDX
    identifier preserved where the author supplied one, and with **no** licence
    applied by us. `attribution` in `NOTICE.md` names the authors.
  * Harness code the VLUN authors wrote for `experiments/` is the authors' own
    work and carries `SPDX-License-Identifier: BUSL-1.1`, which is a
    source-available licence, **not** an OSI open-source licence and **not**
    covered by the licence in part 2. It becomes open source only after the
    Change Date defined in BUSL-1.1; until then, treat it as BUSL-1.1.
    **This licence grants no rights over `experiments/`; that tree is not in
    the released set** (see `RELEASE_MANIFEST.json`).
  * Mined passages from third-party audit PDFs and the named individuals'
    contact details inside them are not redistributed. The distribution view
    keeps the pointer, the page locator and the report hash instead.

What we have done, and what we have not:

  DONE    every quoted citation is pinned to an exact commit and carries a sha256,
          so any recipient can identify precisely which bytes were quoted;
  DONE    every record states, in `license_classification` and `redistribution`,
          what the upstream repository declared and whether text was included;
  DONE    some citations were withheld entirely rather than quoted;
  DONE    the released set is enumerated per path in `RELEASE_MANIFEST.json`, so
          "is this file in the release?" is a question with a machine-readable
          answer;
  NOT DONE  we have not obtained redistribution permission from the upstream
          protocol authors, and we cannot.

If you need a clean dataset for redistribution or commercial training, use the
released paths, use the withheld records, or use `scripts/fetch_sources.py` to
pull sources yourself under your own compliance process.

================================================================================
4. ATTRIBUTION  —  AND WHY IT IS NOT A LICENCE
================================================================================

Findings, verdicts and PoC code belong to their original authors: Code4rena and
the competitive-audit participants. See NOTICE.md for the attribution tables and
the per-source pointers.

**Attribution is not a redistribution right.** Naming an author, linking to
their audit, or recording the sha256 of the bytes they published identifies
whose work it is. It does not license it, does not substitute for the terms
they set, and does not become one by being repeated often enough or by being
attributed correctly. Where a source has declared no terms at all, there is
nothing to comply with and nothing that attribution can supply.

The CC BY 4.0 attribution requirement in part 1 runs in addition to, not
instead of, that attribution — and it applies to the project's own material,
not to the third-party material the project is not licensing.

================================================================================
5. THE RELEASE MANIFEST, AND WHAT IS STILL OPEN
================================================================================

`RELEASE_MANIFEST.json` is the authoritative statement of what may be
redistributed. It is generated, reproducible and machine-checked:

    python scripts/build_release_manifest.py --check
    python scripts/check_rights_consistency.py --quiet

Cases this project cannot close mechanically — the upstream terms that are
unstated, the source-available licences, the No-Derivatives term, the personal
data, and the repository documents that still describe the old blanket
licence — are listed with their reasons in the manifest's `open_questions` and
in `NOTICE.md`. They are open questions, not clearances, and they need human
legal review; nobody in this repository is qualified to close them by
assertion.
